      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Foret de Montravail MTB (Martinique, MQ) [shared trails, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Accessible forest trail riding above Sainte-Luce</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>A compact network of maintained forest tracks combines rolling climbs, roots and short natural descents. Martinique Tourism recommends Montravail for intermediate MTB riders, but the forest is shared with walkers and is not a gravity bike park.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>Free pedal access; no lift. Build loops from the forest parking areas and remain on routes where cycling is permitted.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round, with the most reliable conditions December-May. Rain makes roots and tropical clay extremely slippery.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Sainte-Luce</td><td>Car / bike</td><td>~20 min</td></tr><tr><td>Fort-de-France</td><td>Car</td><td>~45 min</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://www.martinique.org/en/blog/activities-sports/mountain-biking-martinique">Martinique Tourism MTB guide</a>. Yield to walkers and respect current forestry notices.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-60.9560,14.4910,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>montravail-martinique</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>beginner expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>12-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>06-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Morne Gommier MTB (Martinique, MQ) [shared tracks, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Panoramic southern Martinique hill riding</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>Steep lanes, farm tracks and natural paths around Morne Gommier form scenic cross-country and short enduro-style loops above Le Marin. The terrain is more mixed-surface exploration than a marked trail centre.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift and no trail pass. Pedal from Le Marin or start near the viewpoint; confirm private-property boundaries and use a local guide for less obvious connections.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Rideable year-round; December-May is generally drier. Avoid exposed routes during heavy rain, tropical storms and the hottest part of the day.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Le Marin</td><td>Bike / car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Fort-de-France</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://www.martinique.org/en/blog/activities-sports/mountain-biking-martinique">Martinique Tourism MTB guide</a>. Route choice and land access should be confirmed locally.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-60.8865,14.4675,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>morne-gommier-martinique</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>12-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>06-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Trace des Jesuites (Martinique, MQ) [shared rainforest trail, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Technical rainforest singletrack in northern Martinique</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>A narrow historic trail crosses dense rainforest with roots, rocks, stream crossings and persistently slippery ground. Martinique Tourism lists it for advanced mountain biking, but it is principally a shared hiking trail rather than a managed MTB route.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Arrange a point-to-point pickup or return by road. Check current bicycle permission and trail condition with local authorities or a guide before riding, then give absolute priority to walkers.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Best attempted in the drier December-May period. Do not ride after heavy rain, during flood risk or when the trail surface would be damaged.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Fort-de-France</td><td>Car</td><td>~35 min</td></tr><tr><td>Morne-Rouge</td><td>Car</td><td>~20 min</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://www.martinique.org/en/blog/activities-sports/mountain-biking-martinique">Martinique Tourism MTB guide</a>. Current access must be checked locally.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-61.0850,14.7050,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>trace-des-jesuites</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>12-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>06-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Bisdary Downhill Track (Guadeloupe, GP) [no lift, free]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Official short downhill trail above Riviere-Sens</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>A blue-marked 500 m descent drops about 70 m through the Bisdary forest to the marina. It shares part of the Tour du Houelmont hiking trail, so control speed and give pedestrians priority. The run is short but is Guadeloupe's clearest officially documented MTB-specific descent.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>Free access; no lift. Pedal or arrange your own vehicle return from Riviere-Sens to Rue des Archives in Bisdary. Parking is unsupervised.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Permanent route, best December-May. Avoid riding during heavy rain, storms or when the shared surface is saturated.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Basse-Terre</td><td>Bike / car</td><td>~15 min</td></tr><tr><td>Pointe-a-Pitre</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://www.lesilesdeguadeloupe.com/en/equipement/bisdary-downhill-track-mtb/">Guadeloupe Islands official trail page</a>, including downloadable route guide.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-61.7128,15.9859,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>bisdary-downhill</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>beginner natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>12-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>06-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>North Basse-Terre MTB (Sainte-Rose, GP) [guided, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Guided tropical trail riding between coast and rainforest</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>Local routes around Sainte-Rose combine sugar-cane tracks, mangrove edges, riverbeds, tropical forest and steeper natural singletrack. The best connections are deliberately not published on standard maps, making this a guided riding zone rather than a signed trail centre.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. VELOC offers mountain-bike rental, delivery and local route advice or accompaniment from Sainte-Rose. Confirm the selected route, difficulty and price directly.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Guiding and rental are listed year-round in 2026; December-May usually offers drier riding. Routes can close after storms, flooding or forestry damage.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Pointe-a-Pitre</td><td>Car</td><td>~35 min</td></tr><tr><td>Deshaies</td><td>Car</td><td>~30 min</td></tr></table><br/><i>Sources: <a href="https://www.lesilesdeguadeloupe.com/commerce-service/veloc-location-de-vtt/">Guadeloupe Islands tourism listing</a> and <a href="https://www.veloc.org/">VELOC</a>.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>-61.6965,16.3310,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>north-basse-terre-mtb</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>beginner expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>12-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>06-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Shymbulak Bike Park (Almaty, KZ) [uplift status unverified]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>High-altitude resort terrain above Almaty</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>Shymbulak's published resort rules define a downhill bike park with purpose-built natural and artificial features, using the steep ski terrain between roughly 2,200 and 3,180 m. However, the official 2026 summer programme does not currently list public MTB operation, so trail maintenance and access must be confirmed before travel.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>The Medeu-Shymbulak gondola and upper lifts operate for summer visitors, but 2026 bicycle carriage, bike-park uplift and prices are unverified. Do not assume a sightseeing ticket includes a bicycle.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; Current bike-park operation is unverified. Contact Shymbulak before planning a riding day.</p><b>Open season</b><br/>Potential summer operation from June through September; snow, maintenance and national-park restrictions apply. <br/>Daily opening hours: 09:00-18:00<br/><small>Summer resort estimate only; bicycle uplift and bike-park operation must be confirmed.</small><br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Central Almaty</td><td>Bus/taxi + gondola</td><td>~45 min</td></tr></table><br/><i>Sources: <a href="https://shymbulak.com/en/info/about/">official resort overview</a> and the <a href="https://storage.yandexcloud.kz/shymbulak/media-test/58b2111d00c198fd54a290b715e88372.pdf">published resort rules</a>.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-blue</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>77.0802,43.1280,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>shymbulak-mtb</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>expert bikepark</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>06-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>10-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Transport</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Kok-Zhailau MTB (Almaty, KZ) [shared trails, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Alpine plateau and ridge riding directly above Almaty</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>Rocky forest tracks climb from the city edge to the Kok-Zhailau plateau around 2,000-2,200 m. Beginner outings turn around on the plateau; experienced riders can continue toward the meteorological station or exposed ridge terrain. These are busy shared mountain trails, not a bike park.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Pedal from Prosveshchenets or the Medeu side, or book a local guided e-MTB outing. Give way to hikers and horses, especially on summer weekends.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Best May-October. Snow, spring mud, thunderstorms and national-park closures affect the higher route.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Central Almaty</td><td>Bike / bus + bike</td><td>~30 min to trailhead</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://tours.xbike.kz/en/guide/kok-zhailau">local Kok-Zhailau cycling guide</a>. Confirm permitted routes locally.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>76.9760,43.1580,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>kok-zhailau</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>beginner expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>05-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>11-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Manali / Solang Valley MTB (Himachal Pradesh, IN) [shuttle, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>India's best-known Himalayan gravity base</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>Steep pine-forest singletrack, village paths and rough alpine tracks descend around Solang, Old Manali and the surrounding Kullu mountains. Terrain ranges from guided cross-country to technical enduro and downhill; it is a developing regional network rather than a signed bike park.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No verified public bike lift. Local operators arrange 4x4 shuttles, guiding and occasional specialist helibiking, all of which must be booked and checked independently. The government has also planned dedicated Manali and Solang cycling trails, but completion status varies.<br/><br/><b>Open season</b><br/>Best April-June and September-November. Monsoon rain, winter snow, landslides and heavy Solang road traffic can prevent riding.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Manali</td><td>Bike / shuttle</td><td>~35 min to Solang</td></tr><tr><td>Kullu airport</td><td>Car</td><td>~1h30</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://himachaltourism.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/8.-DDR-of-Mountain-Biking-Trails-and-EV-HoHo-Buses-F.pdf">Himachal Pradesh mountain-bike trail planning document</a>. Use a current local MTB operator.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>77.1567,32.3160,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
      <Data name="spot"><value>manali-solang</value></Data><Data name="tags"><value>beginner expert natural</value></Data>
      <Data name="open_from"><value>04-01</value></Data><Data name="closed_from"><value>12-01</value></Data>
      <mwm:icon>Mountain</mwm:icon><mwm:visibility>1</mwm:visibility>
    </ExtendedData>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Khardung La / Nubra MTB (Ladakh, IN) [vehicle drop, no lift]</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Extreme-altitude descent from one of Ladakh's great passes</b><br/><br/><b>Trails</b><br/>The classic objective is a supported ride from the Khardung La road pass into the Indus or Nubra side, combining a very long high-altitude road descent with optional rough tracks and village routes. It is an expedition ride, not continuous singletrack or a bike park.<br/><br/><b>Getting up / price</b><br/>No lift. Use a licensed local guide and support vehicle from Leh. Current road permits, checkpoints and route restrictions must be verified; independent foreign travel rules can change.<br/><br/><p class="warn">&#9888;&#65039; The pass is above 5,300 m. Acclimatize in Leh first and treat altitude illness, cold, traffic and remote rescue as serious hazards.</p><b>Open season</b><br/>Usually May-September, entirely dependent on snow clearance, road status and military or civil restrictions.<br/><br/><b>Access</b><table class="access"><tr><th>From</th><th>Transport</th><th>Duration</th></tr><tr><td>Leh</td><td>Guide vehicle</td><td>~2h to pass</td></tr></table><br/><i>Source: <a href="https://leh.nic.in/tourism/adventures/">District Leh adventure tourism guide</a>, which lists Khardung La and Nubra among its mountain-bike areas.</i>]]></description>
    <styleUrl>#placemark-brown</styleUrl>
    <Point><coordinates>77.6046,34.2783,0</coordinates></Point>
    <ExtendedData xmlns:mwm="https://omaps.app">
